   "summary": "External Affairs Minister Dr S Jaishankar on Saturday said that the border with China has become “more alive in a way” and added that India's capability along the frontier has significantly improved. Alluding to Galwan, Jaishankar, speaking at the 24th Hindustan Times Leadership Summit in New Delhi, said: “Our capability to sustain ourselves and deploy forces along that border has improved.",
   "summary_zh": "外交部长 S Jaishankar 博士周六表示，与中国的边境已经“以某种方式变得更加活跃”，并补充说，印度在边境沿线的能力已显著提升。Jaishankar 在新德里举行的第24届 Hindustan Times Leadership Summit 上谈及 Galwan 时表示：“我们维持自身运转并向边境沿线部署力量的能力已经得到提升。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "External Affairs Minister Dr S Jaishankar on Saturday said that the border with China has become “more alive in a way” and added that India's capability along the frontier has significantly improved. Alluding to Galwan, Jaishankar, speaking at the 24th Hindustan Times Leadership Summit in New Delhi, said: “Our capability to sustain ourselves and deploy forces along that border has improved. I would say it has improved in a very significant way.” Track the HTLS 2026 live here The bilateral ties between the two countries had plummeted to a new low in 2020 following violent clash at Galwan Valley in June that left 20 Indian soldiers and an unspecified number of Chinese troops. He said that India and China’s economies were of the same stature in the 1980s but while Beijing pushed towards industrialisation, New Delhi “missed the bus”. “When Rajiv Gandhi went to China in 1988, India and China’s economies were the same size. But China in the 1980s made a huge push towards modernization and industrialization. I'm sorry to say India in the 1980s simply missed the bus,” he said",
