   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/business/auto-stocks-skid-1-on-profit-booking-despite-softer-crude-firm-markets/article71693492.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:21:18 +0530",
   "_dt": 1794484278.0,
   "ageDays": 1.88,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription The Nifty Auto index fell over 1% on Thursday despite easing crude oil prices and a broadly positive trend in the domestic equity market, as investors booked profits following the previous session’s rally. Analysts attributed Thursday’s reversal largely to profit booking. The Nifty IT index also remained under pressure, extending its decline to a third consecutive session despite a recovery in global AI and technology stocks.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅后即可解锁。尽管原油价格回落、国内股市整体呈积极态势，Nifty Auto指数周四仍下跌逾1%，原因是投资者在前一交易日上涨后获利了结。分析人士将周四的走势逆转主要归因于获利回吐。尽管全球人工智能和科技股有所回升，Nifty IT指数仍承受压力，连续第三个交易日下跌。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The Nifty Auto index fell over 1% on Thursday despite easing crude oil prices and a broadly positive trend in the domestic equity market, as investors booked profits following the previous session’s rally. The sectoral index shed 292 points, or 1.02%, to close at 28,231. In contrast, the benchmark Nifty 50 and Sensex gained 44 points and 32 points, respectively, to settle at 25,299.35 and 81,911.01. Among auto constituents, Hyundai Motor India was the biggest loser, declining nearly 2.5%, followed by Mahindra & Mahindra, which fell 1.9%, and Tata Motors, down 1.3%. The index had rallied on Wednesday, supported by a decline in crude oil prices. Analysts attributed Thursday’s reversal largely to profit booking. The Nifty IT index also remained under pressure, extending its decline to a third consecutive session despite a recovery in global AI and technology stocks. “Global AI stock recovery is likely to continue in the near term. The sharp corrections seen globally last week have subdued following the sweeping victory of the Democrats. It could be unrealistic to expect further sharp decline, but the Nvidia issue could weigh,” said VK Vijayakumar, chief investment strategist at Geojit Financial Services. Meanwhile, the rupee surrendered its early gains from foreign fund inflows as state-run banks purchased dollars on behalf of oil marketing companies, while concerns over rising inflation added to pressure. The currency closed at 94.04 against the US dollar, down 0.4% from its previous close. “Closing near 94.0425, the pair reflects a clear tug-of-war where broad US dollar strength and elevated Fed rate expectations have temporarily overshadowed India’s supportive local equity inflows and easing oil costs,” said Anil Kumar Bhansali, head of treasury, Finrex Treasury Advisors LLP. Adding to currency concerns, India’s consumer price inflation rose to a two-year high of 6.09% in October, breaching the Reserve Bank of India’s upper tolerance limit of 6%. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
