   "title": "Young India Residential Schools to be ready from next academic year: Telangana Deputy CM",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/telangana/young-india-residential-schools-to-be-ready-from-next-academic-year-telangana-deputy-cm/article71689442.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Wed, 11 Nov 2026 17:17:41 +0530",
   "_dt": 1794397661.0,
   "ageDays": 2.89,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription Telangana Deputy Chief Minister Mallu Bhatti Vikramarka said the State government was making efforts to open several of the 105 Young India Integrated Residential Schools from the next academic year, with construction works progressing at a brisk pace. Referring to the Telangana Public Schools initiative, the Deputy Chief Minister said one school had been sanctioned for each Assembly constituency.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁这些内容。Telangana（特伦甘纳邦）副首席部长马卢·巴蒂·维克拉马尔卡表示，邦政府正努力从下一学年开始开放105所“青年印度综合寄宿学校”中的数所，目前施工进展迅速。副首席部长在谈及“Telangana（特伦甘纳邦）公立学校”计划时表示，每个选区均获批建设一所学校。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Telangana Deputy Chief Minister Mallu Bhatti Vikramarka said the State government was making efforts to open several of the 105 Young India Integrated Residential Schools from the next academic year, with construction works progressing at a brisk pace. The government had sanctioned 105 schools across the State at an estimated cost of ₹200 crore each as part of its efforts to provide quality education to children, irrespective of their socio-economic background, he said. Speaking after laying the foundation stone for a Telangana Public School building at the Government Boys’ School in Kazipet on Wednesday (November 11, 2026), under the Warangal West Assembly constituency, Mr. Vikramarka said the government was determined to develop an education system comparable to those in the world’s top 10 developed countries. Referring to the Telangana Public Schools initiative, the Deputy Chief Minister said one school had been sanctioned for each Assembly constituency. These institutions would offer English-medium education from pre-primary to Class 12, with digital classrooms, modern teaching methods, trained teachers and other facilities. The schools would function under a semi-residential model, under which the government would provide uniforms, shoes, textbooks, notebooks, school bags and transportation facilities. Students would receive breakfast and lunch at school and return home in the evening after sports and other activities. Mr. Vikramarka said the model was designed to provide quality education without depriving children of the opportunity to spend time with their families. He said the government had introduced a breakfast scheme for nearly 27 lakh students studying in government schools across the State this year. Providing breakfast to such a large number of students within an hour was a unique initiative, he claimed. Recalling the contributions of India’s first Education Minister Maulana Abul Kalam Azad on his birth anniversary, Mr. Vikramarka said the government was committed to carrying forward his ideals of promoting education and scientific knowledge. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
